course: books
lesson: 76
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 3: How the Rhinoceros got his Skin
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On a lonely island by the Red Sea lives a Parsee man with nothing but a shining hat, a knife and a cooking-stove, and one day he bakes himself a gigantic cake.
explain: Just as he is about to eat it, a rude Rhinoceros, whose skin in those days fitted tight with no wrinkles, stomps up; the man scrambles up a palm tree, and the Rhino knocks over the stove, spikes the cake on his horn and eats it all.
explain: Five weeks later, in a heat wave, the Rhino takes off his skin, which then buttoned underneath like a raincoat, and leaves it on the beach while he bathes.
explain: The Parsee takes his revenge: he rubs the skin full of stale, tickly cake-crumbs, and when the Rhino buttons it back on, it itches so badly that he rubs against the palm tree until his skin is pushed into great folds.
explain: That, says the story, is why rhinos have baggy, folded skin and a very bad temper; it is a funny revenge tale with a little lesson: "Them that takes cakes ... makes dreadful mistakes."
fact: A "Parsee" (today usually spelled Parsi) is one of the Parsi people, whose families came to India from Persia (Iran) long ago; in this story he lives on "an uninhabited island" (an island where nobody lives) on the shores of the Red Sea.
fact: He had only "his hat and his knife and a cooking-stove of the kind that you must particularly never touch", and the sun flashed brightly off his hat.
fact: He made "one cake which was two feet across and three feet thick", from flour, water, currants, plums and sugar; Kipling calls it "a Superior Comestible" ("comestible" = something to eat).
fact: The Rhinoceros came with "a horn on his nose, two piggy eyes, and few manners"; his skin "fitted him quite tight. There were no wrinkles in it anywhere."
fact: The Rhino said "How!", the Parsee climbed a palm tree, and the Rhino "upset the oil-stove with his nose" and "spiked that cake on the horn of his nose, and he ate it", and went away waving his tail, saying nothing whatever.
fact: The Parsee's rhyme: "Them that takes cakes / Which the Parsee-man bakes / Makes dreadful mistakes." ("Them that takes" is old country grammar for "those who take".)
fact: "Five weeks later, there was a heat wave in the Red Sea", and "everybody took off all the clothes they had": the Parsee took off his hat (it was all he wore), but the Rhino took off his skin, which "buttoned underneath with three buttons and looked like a waterproof" (a raincoat); then he went into the sea, blowing bubbles through his nose.
fact: The Parsee came down from his palm tree, "smiled one smile that ran all round his face two times", danced three times round the skin, then filled his hat with crumbs and rubbed the skin full of "old, dry, stale, tickly cake-crumbs and some burned currants". (He never ate anything but cake and never swept out his camp, so there were plenty of crumbs.)
fact: The Rhino "rolled and rolled and rolled", and the more he scratched the worse it tickled; then he rubbed against the palm tree so hard that he made "a great fold over his shoulders, and another fold underneath", rubbed the buttons off, and made more folds over his legs.
fact: The story says every rhinoceros since has "great folds in his skin and a very bad temper, all on account of the cake-crumbs inside".
fact: Real biology: rhino skin is very thick (several centimetres in places) and it grows that way; the deep folds belong most of all to the Indian (greater one-horned) rhino and let it bend and move.
fact: Real biology: rhinos eat only plants (grass, leaves, shoots), never cake; their horns are made of keratin, the same stuff as hair and fingernails.
fact: Real biology: rhinos have poor eyesight but a very good sense of smell and hearing, which may be why some (like the black rhino) charge at things that startle them.
fact: Real biology: rhinos wallow in mud, which keeps them cool and protects their skin from sunburn and biting insects, so a real rhino would not "take off his skin" to cool down.
fact: The poem at the end says the island is "off Cape Gardafui, / By the Beaches of Socotra": real places at the tip of the Horn of Africa.
exercise: Where did the Parsee live? | On an uninhabited island by the Red Sea | In the middle of a Howling Desert | On a raft at Fifty North and Forty West | By the banks of a great grey river
exercise: What does "uninhabited" mean? | Nobody lives there | It is very hot | It has no trees | It is very small and flat
exercise: Which things did the Parsee own? | His hat, his knife and a cooking-stove | A raft, a pair of braces and a knife | A saddle, a yoke and a long stick | A boat, a net and a fishing hook
exercise: How big was the cake? | Two feet across and three feet thick | Small enough to fit inside his hat | As big as the palm tree he climbed | Three feet across and two feet thick
exercise: What does "Comestible" mean? | Something to eat | Something to wear | A kind of stove | A magic spell
exercise: How did the Rhinoceros's skin fit him in those days? | Quite tight, with no wrinkles | Very loose, with big baggy folds | Like a coat with a hood | Full of spots and stripes
exercise: Which words describe the Rhino when he first came? | A horn on his nose, two piggy eyes and few manners | A long neck, long legs and a very proud back | A long trunk, big ears and a lot of questions | Four short legs, grey wool and very great pride
exercise: What did the Rhino say when he arrived? | "How!" | "Humph!" | "Hello!" | "Cake!"
exercise: What did the Parsee do when the Rhino came? | Climbed to the top of a palm tree | Hid inside his big cooking-stove oven | Threw his knife at the Rhino | Ran into the sea to swim
exercise: How did the Rhino pick up the cake? | He spiked it on the horn of his nose | He lifted it up with his long grey trunk | He rolled it over with his tail | He bit it out of the stove door
exercise: What did the Rhino say about the cake afterwards? | Nothing whatever | "Thank you very much" | "It was too dry" | "Bake me another"
exercise: How much later did the heat wave come? | Five weeks | Five days | Three weeks | One year
exercise: What did the Parsee take off in the heat wave? | His hat | His coat | His boots | His skin
exercise: How did the Rhino's skin fasten in those days? | With three buttons underneath | With a long zip down his back | With a belt around his tummy | With string tied round his legs
exercise: What did the skin look like? | A waterproof (a raincoat) | A big woolly blanket | A suit of shiny metal armour | A sack full of potatoes
exercise: What did the Rhino do in the water? | Blew bubbles through his nose | Swam out far to an island | Caught little fish with his horn | Fell asleep on his back
exercise: What did the Parsee fill the skin with? | Old, dry, stale, tickly cake-crumbs and burned currants | Sand and little stones and pricking thorns from the beach | Ants and beetles and tiny crabs that pinch and bite | Salt water from the Red Sea, mixed up with some sugar
exercise: Why did the Parsee have so many crumbs? | He ate nothing but cake and never swept his camp | He had baked a second cake just for this trick | He had saved them for a whole year in a jar | The Rhino had dropped them all over the beach
exercise: How did the skin feel when the Rhino put it back on? | It tickled like cake crumbs in bed | It was cold and wet from the salty sea | It was much too small and tight | It smelt of smoke from the stove
exercise: What happened to the buttons? | He rubbed them off | The Parsee cut them off | They fell into the sea | They are still there today
exercise: Where were the new folds? | Over his shoulders, underneath, and over his legs | Only on his nose, all round the bottom of his horn | On his ears and his tail, which grew very long | Across his back in stripes, like a zebra in a forest
exercise: According to the story, why do rhinos have a very bad temper? | The cake-crumbs inside their skin still tickle | The Parsee shouted at them from the palm tree | It is far too hot for them in the Red Sea sun | They never got a second slice of the big cake
exercise: Real biology: what are rhino horns made of? | Keratin, like hair and nails | Bone, just like a cow's horns | Ivory, like elephant tusks | Wood, like a tree branch
exercise: Real biology: what do rhinos eat? | Plants such as grass and leaves | Cakes, bread and sweet currants | Small animals and big beetles | Fish they catch with their horns
exercise: Real biology: why do rhinos wallow in mud? | To keep cool and protect their skin from sun and insects | To scratch out the cake-crumbs that tickle inside them | To hide from the people who live on the islands there | To find the fish and frogs that they like to eat most
exercise: Thinking back: in the Camel story, who gave the Camel his hump? | The Djinn in charge of All Deserts | The Man who owned the Horse and Ox | The Parsee from the Red Sea island | The 'Stute Fish behind the Whale's ear
exercise: Thinking back: what did the Mariner use to tie the grating? | His suspenders | His breeches | His hat | A rope of seaweed
test: Ella reckons: "The Parsee was just mean for no reason." Put her right. | The Rhino had knocked over his stove and eaten his cake | The Rhino had stolen his shining hat and his sharp knife | The Rhino had made him work double-time all week long | The Rhino had swallowed his raft and his blue trousers
test: Josh, London: "That rhino's well peak, bruv, ate the man's whole cake, no ask." ("peak" = unfair and bad luck, "bruv" = mate.) Which word does the story use for him? | He had few manners | He was most astute | He was very idle | He was very curious
test: What would have happened if the Rhino had kept his skin on in the heat wave? | The Parsee could not have filled it with crumbs | The Rhino would have melted away in the sun | The Parsee would have baked him another cake | The skin would have grown its folds by itself
test: Chloe, Gen Z: "The Parsee's smile went round his face TWICE, I'm dead." ("I'm dead" = that's so funny.) Why was he so happy? | He had found the Rhino's skin and saw his chance | He had baked a brand-new cake even bigger | The Rhino had said sorry for eating up all the cake | He had seen a ship come to take him home
test: True or false: rhinos really can take their skin off and button it back on. | False: it is a made-up joke; rhino skin grows on them | True: they do it in every heat wave to keep cool | True: but only the Indian rhino has the buttons | False: only baby rhinos can take their skin off
test: Uncle Mike: "Them that takes cakes... d'ya get it?" What is the rhyme really saying? | If you steal, you will be paid back for it | Cakes are bad for you, so do not eat them | Parsee-men bake the best cakes of all | Only rhinos make mistakes about cakes
test: Big sis, sarcastic: "Oh sure, scratching makes an itch go away, genius." What does the story say happened when the Rhino scratched? | It made it worse | It cured the itch | It woke the Parsee | It tore his skin
test: Tom says rhinos have folded skin because of cake-crumbs. What would a zookeeper say? | The folds grow naturally and help the rhino bend and move | Yes, zookeepers still find the crumbs inside rhino skins | The folds are made by rhinos rubbing against palm trees | The folds come from years of sleeping in tight places
test: Old farmer in Somerset: "Gurt big rhino, eh? Proper job that cake were." ("gurt" = great, big.) How thick was the cake? | Three feet | Two feet | One foot | Five feet high
test: In all three stories so far, an animal does something wrong and is changed for ever. Which animal did what? | Whale ate too much; Camel was idle; Rhino stole a cake | Whale was idle; Camel stole a cake; Rhino ate too much | Whale stole a cake; Camel ate too much; Rhino was idle | All three were simply rude to a clever little fish
test: The Mariner made a rhyme after his trick, and so did the Parsee. What do both rhymes do? | Tease the animal they have just tricked | Ask the animal to say it is sorry | Thank a magic Djinn for their help | Tell the animal to come back again very soon
test: Aisha, Singapore: "The Rhino so rude one, take cake also never say sorry, lah." ("lah" just adds feeling.) Which other animal in the book so far was also rude and never learned to behave? | The Camel | The Horse | The 'Stute Fish | The Dog
test: Which character in the first two stories was really clever, like the Parsee? | The Mariner, a man of infinite-resource-and-sagacity | The Camel, who tricked the Horse and the Dog and the Ox | The Whale, who found a man on a raft in the middle of the sea | The Ox, who went straight to the Djinn of All Deserts
test: ★★★ The cake was "two feet across and three feet thick". Which is true? | It was taller than it was wide | It was wider than it was tall | It was the same height and width | It was smaller than the Parsee's hat
test: ★★★ Count the Parsee's moves in the right order after he found the skin. Which order is right? | Smiled, danced three times round it, filled his hat with crumbs, rubbed them in | Danced, filled his hat with crumbs, smiled, climbed the tree, rubbed them in | Filled his hat with crumbs, smiled, danced, rubbed them in, came down the tree | Smiled, rubbed in the crumbs, danced round it, filled his hat with the crumbs
test: ★★★ Trap in the wording: in the heat wave "everybody took off all the clothes they had". What did the Parsee take off? | Only his hat, since it was all he wore | His skin, just like the hungry Rhino did | Nothing at all, as he stayed in the shade | His coat, his hat and his heavy brown boots
exam: Identify the item consumed without permission by the Rhinoceros upon his first appearance. | A cake measuring two feet across and three feet thick | A quantity of fish caught within the waters of the Red Sea | The hat of the Parsee, from which sunlight was reflected | A cooking-stove of the kind that must never be touched
exam: Ascertain the substance introduced by the Parsee into the skin of the Rhinoceros. | Stale cake-crumbs and burned currants | Fine sand gathered from the beaches | Salt drawn from the waters of the sea | Thorns of the tamarisk and milkweed
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the origin of the folds in the skin of the Rhinoceros according to the narrative. | Vigorous rubbing against a palm tree to relieve itching | The removal of the skin by the Parsee with his knife | Prolonged immersion in the warm waters of the Red Sea | The weight of the cake upon the horn of the animal
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the material of which the horn of a rhinoceros is composed. | Keratin, the substance of hair and nails | Bone, the substance of a cow's horn | Ivory, the substance of an elephant's tusk | Wood, the substance of a palm tree's trunk
